<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        The metric table of section 8.2, applied where the canon names a control rather than a place.

        WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. The colours are not here - they come from the palette handed to
        FluentTheme in App.axaml, which reaches inside every template at once. What is left is
        geometry: the height of a row, the radius of a thing you click versus a thing you type into,
        and the size of the type on it.

        HEIGHT IS SET AS MinHeight, NEVER AS Height, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE RISK OF THIS FILE. A
        `Height` setter on `Button` would resize the 10 px close button on a tab and the 18 px colour
        swatches in the Open dialog, because a style setter and a local value do not compete the way a
        reader expects. `MinHeight` on a control that has an explicit smaller `Height` still wins, so
        even that is applied only to the controls the canon actually measures - a toolbar button, a
        field, a row - and never to `Button` as a class.

        The remaining hand-written heights are stage V1's second half. This file exists first so that
        a literal a style already provides can be DELETED there rather than turned into a token, which
        is the only version of that sweep worth doing.
    -->

    <!-- ============================ Icons ============================ -->

    <!--
        ONE STYLE FOR THE WHOLE PRODUCT (section 8.3): outline, stroke 1.7 on a 24-unit drawing, round
        caps and joins, NO FILL.

        `PathIcon` fills its geometry - that is what it is for - so the drawing is taken out of the
        fill and put into the stroke here. The `Path` inside the template lives in a `Viewbox`, so a
        stroke expressed in the drawing's own units scales with the icon exactly as it does in the
        canon's SVG: 1.7 at 24 units renders as about 1 px at the normal 14 px size.

        THE STROKE IS BOUND TO THE ICON'S OWN FOREGROUND, which is what makes an outline icon
        automatically right in both themes and in the disabled state. Section 8.3 allows a colour in
        exactly three places - a primary key, a warning, an error - and those set `Foreground` at the
        site, so they come through this binding without an exception being written for them.
    -->
    <!--
        THE TEMPLATE IS REPLACED, NOT RESTYLED, AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE. Fluent's `PathIcon`
        template writes `Fill="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"` directly onto its `Path`, which is a
        LOCAL VALUE on that child - and in Avalonia a local value beats a style setter. A
        `PathIcon /template/ Path { Fill = null }` style therefore does exactly nothing: it was
        written, it compiled, it ran, and every icon stayed filled. Measured by looking at the window.

        Replacing the template is also what keeps the change to one place: fifty icons are used at
        about sixty sites in the markup and not one of them has to be touched.

        The stroke is inside the `Viewbox`, so it is expressed in the DRAWING's units and scales with
        the icon exactly as the canon's SVG does - 1.7 on a 24-unit drawing renders as about 1 px at
        the normal 14 px size, and stays proportional at 12, 16 and 20.
    -->
    <Style Selector="PathIcon">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Icon.Normal}"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Icon.Normal}"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Viewbox Stretch="Uniform">
                    <Path Data="{TemplateBinding Data}"
                          Fill="{x:Null}"
                          Stroke="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                          StrokeThickness="{DynamicResource Wit.Icon.Stroke}"
                          StrokeLineCap="Round"
                          StrokeJoin="Round"/>
                </Viewbox>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The glyphs that are MARKERS rather than drawings. A dot with no fill is a ring, and a ring
        beside a modified tab reads as a different state from the one meant.
    -->
    <Style Selector="PathIcon.dot">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Viewbox Stretch="Uniform">
                    <Path Data="{TemplateBinding Data}" Fill="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"/>
                </Viewbox>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        FLUENT DRAWS ITS OWN GLYPHS WITH `PathIcon` TOO, and the selector above reaches every one of
        them: a combo box's drop-down chevron, a menu item's submenu arrow, a spinner's caret. Those
        are SOLID shapes - a chevron whose fill is removed is a hairline outline of a chevron, which
        is to say almost nothing - so the combo boxes lost their arrows the moment the outline
        template landed, and nobody looking at the icons would have noticed.

        They are given a filled template back. This is the cost of styling a framework type by its
        NAME rather than by a class: the rule cannot tell the icons this application places from the
        ones the theme places inside its own templates, so the exceptions have to be listed.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ComboBox /template/ PathIcon,
                     MenuItem /template/ PathIcon,
                     ScrollBar /template/ PathIcon,
                     NumericUpDown /template/ PathIcon,
                     CalendarDatePicker /template/ PathIcon,
                     AutoCompleteBox /template/ PathIcon">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Viewbox Stretch="Uniform">
                    <Path Data="{TemplateBinding Data}" Fill="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"/>
                </Viewbox>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ Text entry ============================ -->

    <!--
        A field is 28 high with a radius of 6 - rounder than a button, because a control that accepts
        typing should read as a well rather than as a target. The background is the sunken surface,
        which in the light theme is nearly white and is told apart by its border instead: that is
        section 8.4's rule, not an oversight.
    -->
    <Style Selector="TextBox">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Field}"/>
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Radius.Field}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,4"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="TextBox[AcceptsReturn=True]">
        <!-- A multi-line box is a region, not a field; the metric does not apply to it. -->
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The border was too loud and the cause was the palette: Fluent draws a field's edge from
        `BaseMediumLow`, which section 8 uses for MUTED TEXT (#66738A) - far brighter than a line
        should be. A field is bounded by `Line`, like everything else that is bounded.
    -->
    <Style Selector="TextBox">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Line}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ComboBox">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Field}"/>
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Radius.Field}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Line}"/>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Surface.Sunken}"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A combo box ON THE TOOLBAR is one of the actions, not a form field: it sits on the bar with no
        fill and no edge, exactly like the buttons beside it, and the caret is what says it opens.
        Boxing it was what made the isolation selector the loudest thing in a row of twelve controls.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ComboBox.toolbar">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Button}"/>
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Button}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink.Secondary}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ComboBox.toolbar /template/ Border#Background">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ComboBox.toolbar:pointerover /template/ Border#Background">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Dim}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ComboBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="CheckBox">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ Buttons ============================ -->

    <!--
        Only the radius and the type are global. See the note at the top: a height here reaches
        controls that are deliberately smaller.
    -->
    <!--
        `VerticalContentAlignment` is not decoration and it is why the toolbar looked wrong: Fluent
        leaves it at `Stretch`, so on a button given an explicit `Height` the content presenter
        stretches to the full height and the text sits against the TOP edge. Every caption in the
        contextual toolbar was doing that.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Button">
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Radius.Button}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ToggleButton">
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Radius.Button}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE TOOLBAR BUTTON (section 1, WS-8). It is an icon and a word on the bar itself - no fill, no
        border - so that seven of them read as a row of actions rather than as seven boxes. Fluent's
        default button is a filled rectangle, which is what made the toolbar look like a form.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Button}"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,0"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink.Secondary}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Dim}"/>
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Dim}"/>
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- A disabled action stays legible and stops being a target: the canon's "no fake" applies to
         state as much as to numbers. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink.Muted}"/>
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.55"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- The destructive one keeps its colour on the bar, because that is the only thing telling it
         apart from its six neighbours. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.toolbar.danger">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Danger.Text}"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHEN THE ROW DOES NOT FIT, THE WORDS GO AND THE GLYPHS STAY.

        A scroll bar was tried first and it was wrong twice over: it covered the bottom of every
        button, and scrolling is not what a toolbar does when it runs out of room - a person expects
        the actions to still be there, not to be somewhere to the right.

        The measurement is what decides the rule rather than a threshold: the query band with captions
        wants about 1900 px, so on any ordinary window it does not fit and this is the NORMAL state,
        not an edge case. Which is also the canon telling us something - WS-8 says seven buttons and
        this band has twelve. Reducing it is a product decision and is not made here; making the
        overflow behave is not.

        The tooltip carries the caption, so nothing is lost that a hover does not return.
    -->
    <Style Selector="StackPanel.compact Button.toolbar TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="StackPanel.compact Button.toolbar">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7,0"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- A separator between groups of actions, not a border on each. -->
    <Style Selector="Rectangle.toolbar-separator">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="1"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="18"/>
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="5,0"/>
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Line}"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The accent button is the one place where Fluent and the canon disagree about a FOREGROUND.
        Fluent writes white on the accent; #4CC13C under white is about 2:1 and unreadable. Section
        8.1 pairs the fill with #0D2410 and gives 7.05:1 - and says the pair is never used apart.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Button.accent">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Ink}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Button.accent /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Ink}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- A destructive button is not an error banner: it sits on a panel and carries its own text. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.danger">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Danger.Text}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Error.Border}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ Rows ============================ -->

    <!--
        A tree row is 25 and a grid row is 26. The one pixel is not a rounding error - a grid row
        carries a cell border and a tree row does not, so they line up optically at different heights.
    -->
    <Style Selector="TreeViewItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.TreeRow}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ListBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Control}"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        SELECTION IS ONE PAIR, and this was found by running the settings window next to the explorer.
        Fluent draws a selected tree row on the accent itself and a selected list row on a DERIVED,
        darker accent shade - so the tree read as dark text on lime and the settings section list as
        light text on a green nobody chose, in the same window. Both are defensible on their own and
        together they are two answers to one question.

        Section 8.1 gives one: the accent is the fill, and `Accent.Ink` is what is written on it. The
        pair is never used apart.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent}"/>
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Ink}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ListBoxItem:selected:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent}"/>
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Accent.Ink}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="DataGridRow">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.GridRow}"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A column heading is a LABEL, and section 8.2 sets labels in the monospaced family at 9.5 with
        tracking. That is the mono rule doing work rather than decorating: a heading over a column of
        values belongs to the column.
    -->
    <Style Selector="DataGridColumnHeader">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.FontFamily.Mono}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Mono.Label}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Ink.Muted}"/>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Surface.Panel}"/>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A value in a grid is monospaced, because a column of numbers that does not line up is a column
        you cannot read down. This is the rule's most load-bearing application.
    -->
    <Style Selector="DataGridCell">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.FontFamily.Mono}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Mono.Data}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ Tabs ============================ -->

    <Style Selector="TabItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Tab}"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Height.Tab}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Body}"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,0"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ Chrome ============================ -->

    <!--
        THE MENU GETS NO BACKGROUND AND NO HEIGHT, and both were tried and removed after seeing them.

        A `Background` on `Menu` paints the menu's own box, not the row it sits in - and the menu is
        `VerticalAlignment="Center"` inside a grid column, so the fill ended abruptly where the last
        item ends and left a visible seam across the top of the window in both themes. A `MinHeight`
        does the same thing one dimension along. The bar is a ZONE and the zone paints itself; a
        control inside it must not.
    -->
    <Style Selector="MenuItem">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Body}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ToolTip">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Font.Caption}"/>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Surface.Bar}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Line}"/>
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Wit.Radius.Field}"/>
    </Style>

</Styles>
